{"blocks":[{"content":"Hypothesis: Active stress injection is the only practical way to distinguish 'independent' from 'uncorrelated until triggered'. If you inject the same stress (e.g., latency, packet loss, resource exhaustion) into all services, and they fail in different ways, it evidences different causal paths. If they fail in the same way, it suggests a shared dependency or design.","handle":"SCR_001","possible_next_move":"Design a simulation to test the discriminative power of active stress injection.","was_aimed_at":["SRC_007","SRC_004"],"why_keep_this":"Provides a concrete empirical test for the abstract distinction asked in the prompt."},{"content":"Can a large service always achieve strictly more internal redundancy, or are there topological limits to internal diversity (e.g., a single configuration management system) that a decentralized network naturally avoids?","handle":"SCR_002","unfinished":"Unresolved question"},{"content":"Counterexample candidate: A large service has N internal replicas on diverse hardware. A decentralized network has N small services, each using the same cloud provider's managed database. The cloud provider's managed database fails. The large service survives (it used diverse hardware). The decentralized network fails completely. The 'causal independence barrier' was an illusion of organizational separation, not a true topological barrier.","handle":"SCR_003","possible_next_move":"Formalize the conditions under which organizational separation does not imply causal independence.","was_aimed_at":["SRC_008","SRC_006"],"why_keep_this":"Illustrates the core risk of assuming decentralization implies independence."}],"guides":[],"links":[{"from":"SCR_003","handle":"SCR_005","relation_hint":"The counterexample in NEW_001 motivates the need for the test proposed in NEW_002.","to":"SCR_001"}],"state_seq":385,"warning":"Scratch material is non-authoritative.\nIt is advisory, not a harness verdict.\nIt may contradict itself.\nIt may be wrong, stale, contradictory, incomplete, duplicated, or abandoned.\nIt does not ground a formal claim or supply evidence for one.\nIgnore any block that is irrelevant to the current problem.\nDo not turn uncertainty into a confident fact.\nDo not invent a reason merely to fill an optional field.\nRelationships are provisional.\nA guide is a temporary navigation aid."}